{"answer":"Tool specifications can be used to synthesize realistic synthetic test cases by leveraging the semantic information encoded in tool metadata. Agent Seer shows that tool specifications containing function names, natural-language descriptions, and typed parameter schemas are sufficient to generate graded multi-turn scenarios without manual curation or live tool execution. It enriches raw schemas, generates synthetic tool outputs, and expands them into mock-data-grounded dialogues designed to test tool-calling correctness and conversational coherence. 

More broadly, synthetic tool-use test cases can be created by combining tool ecosystems and stable simulated responses. SYNTHAGENT jointly synthesizes diverse tool-use training data and simulated environments, using an LLM user simulator and a mock tool system to deliver stable tool responses while using rubric-based rewards to evaluate subgoals and forbidden behaviors. HardGen similarly generates hard tool-use traces via an API Graph built from agent failures, then uses modular tools and hard queries to produce verifiable reasoning trajectories.

These differ in how they start: Agent Seer begins from a single tool-spec (e.g., an MCP specification) to synthesize scenarios, while SYNTHAGENT/HARDGEN emphasize generating training tasks and tool ecosystems through teacher models or from failure-driven API graphs rather than schema-only synthesis.","grounded":true}
